READINESS_BODY = '''
<section class="section"><div class="wrap grid-2"><div><span class="badge">Decision trigger</span><h2>The system can demonstrate value, but the release evidence is incomplete.</h2><p>This review is designed for founders, CTOs, product owners, risk leaders and operating teams preparing to give an AI system real responsibility. It tests whether the evidence supports that decision. The work does not assume that a successful demo proves reliability, security, authority or recoverability.</p><p>A useful starting point is a named system, intended users, business consequence, current release stage and the evidence already available. The review then separates what is observed from what the team believes, and turns missing evidence into owned actions.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Five decision domains</h3><ol><li>Outcome and evaluation evidence</li><li>Authority and human oversight</li><li>Data, security and supplier boundaries</li><li>Failure response, rollback and ownership</li><li>Consumption, cost and operating viability</li></ol></div></div></section>
<section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><span class="badge">Method</span><h2>Four stages create a defensible release record.</h2><div class="grid-2"><article class="card"><h3>1. Frame the decision</h3><p>Document the intended outcome, users, system boundary, material consequences and the exact decision due. A readiness review fails when it becomes an open-ended architecture assessment, so scope is tied to one release or responsibility change.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>2. Inspect the evidence</h3><p>Trace requirements into evaluations, test summaries, access controls, data flows, vendor dependencies, incident procedures and operating owners. Evidence is labelled current, stale, missing or not applicable.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>3. Challenge failure and recovery</h3><p>Review selected failure modes, unsupported answers, tool misuse, escalation, human override and rollback using agreed safe inputs. This is not a penetration test and does not replace specialist security assurance.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>4. Decide and assign</h3><p>Issue a release, remediate or hold recommendation with conditions, owners, evidence requests and a 30-day action plan. The recommendation states limitations and unresolved dependencies.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap grid-2"><div><span class="badge">Named deliverables</span><h2>What the buyer receives.</h2><ul><li>System and decision boundary</li><li>Source-linked evidence register</li><li>Evaluation and failure-mode gaps</li><li>Agent/tool authority matrix</li><li>Human review and escalation matrix</li><li>Data and supplier dependency map</li><li>Incident and rollback readiness record</li><li>Release conditions and 30-day plan</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h3>Representative deliverable</h3><p>The public portfolio shows a redacted production-readiness decision record built from this website's own release controls. It is self-tested evidence and is not presented as a customer outcome.</p><a class="button secondary" href="/portfolio/production-ai-readiness-sample/">View the sample dossier</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><h2>What this review does not claim.</h2><p>It is not a certification, legal opinion, compliance attestation, penetration test, model-safety guarantee or substitute for the accountable system owner. It does not prove an AI system is safe under every condition. It creates an evidence-backed decision record for a defined release question.</p><p>Teams that already have an independent evaluation function, complete release evidence and named operating ownership may not need this service. Teams seeking a broad transformation program, continuous managed operations or regulated legal advice require a different scope or specialist partner.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap"><span class="badge">Buyer questions</span><h2>Questions the diagnostic is designed to answer.</h2><div class="grid-2"><article class="card"><h3>How do we know the pilot is ready?</h3><p>A pilot is not ready because it completed a demonstration. The decision needs agreed success and failure criteria, representative evaluation evidence, an accountable owner, human authority boundaries, incident response and a workable rollback path. The diagnostic identifies which evidence exists and which release conditions remain open.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What if the AI agent can take actions?</h3><p>The review maps each tool, data source and consequential action to allowed scope, authentication, approval, logging, exception handling and human override. It tests the written boundary against available implementation evidence without requesting production credentials through the public intake.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Can this support procurement or board review?</h3><p>The dossier is structured for decision-makers: the system boundary, evidence register, major gaps, accountable owners, conditions and limitations are explicit. It can support an internal review, but it does not replace a required audit, legal opinion or regulated certification.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What happens after a hold decision?</h3><p>A hold is translated into an ordered remediation record. Each action includes the missing evidence, responsible owner, dependency and the check that would allow the decision to be reconsidered. This prevents a vague risk concern from becoming an indefinite project.</p></article></div></div></section>'''

ECON_BODY = '''
<section class="section"><div class="wrap grid-2"><div><span class="badge">Decision trigger</span><h2>Spend is rising faster than accountable value.</h2><p>This review is for founders, finance partners, engineering leaders, platform owners and product teams who can see cloud or AI cost increasing but cannot explain it by workload, product, customer, model or owner. The goal is an action-ready economic baseline, not a generic savings percentage.</p><p>The review starts with billing and usage exports, workload maps, commercial assumptions and owner interviews supplied through an agreed secure route. It does not require production credentials through the public website.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Four economics questions</h3><ol><li>What is being consumed?</li><li>Who owns the decision?</li><li>What unit of value should cost follow?</li><li>Which action is safe now?</li></ol></div></div></section>
<section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><span class="badge">Method</span><h2>From invoice lines to owned decisions.</h2><div class="grid-2"><article class="card"><h3>1. Establish the baseline</h3><p>Normalize the relevant cloud, model, GPU, data and platform cost into a dated scope. Separate recurring consumption, committed spend, idle capacity, transfer, storage and third-party AI charges.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>2. Map cost to owners and value</h3><p>Connect major cost pools to a workload, product, environment, business owner and useful value unit where evidence permits. Unallocated spend remains visible rather than being forced into a misleading model.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>3. Test action choices</h3><p>Compare rightsizing, scheduling, architecture, model choice, caching, retention, commitment and product-policy actions. Each action records expected benefit, risk, dependency, owner and verification method.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>4. Create the control loop</h3><p>Define thresholds, anomaly ownership, approval gates and review cadence so the first saving does not disappear after the report. Forecasts are labelled assumptions until observed.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap grid-2"><div><span class="badge">Named deliverables</span><h2>What the buyer receives.</h2><ul><li>Dated cost and allocation baseline</li><li>Workload and owner map</li><li>Unit-economics decision model</li><li>Waste and uncertainty register</li><li>Prioritized action backlog</li><li>Approval and verification plan</li><li>30-day operating scorecard</li><li>Executive decision readout</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h3>Representative deliverable</h3><p>The public portfolio includes a transparent cloud-AI economics model using synthetic values. It demonstrates the structure and formulas without claiming customer savings.</p><a class="button secondary" href="/portfolio/cloud-ai-economics-sample/">View the sample model</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><h2>What this review does not claim.</h2><p>It does not guarantee a saving percentage, replace cloud-provider support, execute production shutdowns, purchase commitments or certify financial statements. Results depend on the quality of billing exports, ownership data, architecture context and the team's authority to implement actions.</p><p>Teams with mature allocation, active FinOps governance and reliable unit economics may need a targeted engineering engagement rather than this baseline review.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap"><span class="badge">Buyer questions</span><h2>Questions the economics review is designed to answer.</h2><div class="grid-2"><article class="card"><h3>Why did cloud or AI cost increase?</h3><p>The review separates growth caused by useful demand from idle capacity, architecture choices, model routing, data retention, transfer, experimentation and unallocated ownership. A cost increase is not labelled waste until the workload and value context are understood.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Which cost action is safe first?</h3><p>Candidate actions are ranked by evidence, expected value, implementation effort, service risk, data risk and reversibility. Shutting down or committing spend solely from a billing chart is excluded. The responsible workload owner must accept the action and verification rule.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>How should AI unit economics be measured?</h3><p>The useful unit depends on the product: a completed workflow, evaluated answer, resolved case, active customer or another business event. The model documents the numerator, denominator, exclusions and data gaps so finance and engineering use the same definition.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Can this replace a FinOps platform?</h3><p>No. A platform may supply allocation, anomaly and commitment data. The review is useful when owners still need to choose the decision model, evidence boundary and action sequence. Mature teams may move directly to targeted engineering or platform operations.</p></article></div></div></section>'''

